.Dd October 15, 2026
.Dt FFS 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ffs
.Nd search files for a pattern, with ripgrep's command line
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar options
.Ar pattern
.Op Ar path ...
.Nm
.Op Ar options
.Fl e Ar pattern ...
.Op Ar path ...
.Nm
.Fl -files
.Op Ar options
.Op Ar path ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
prints the lines of files that match
.Ar pattern ,
a regular expression.
Its options and output are those of
.Xr rg 1
for the options it has, so it can stand in for ripgrep in scripts and in
what coding agents run.
.Pp
Each
.Ar path
is a directory, searched recursively, a file, or
.Sq -
for standard input.
With no
.Ar path ,
.Nm
searches standard input if it is a pipe or a file, and the current directory
otherwise.
.Pp
Searching a directory skips hidden files and directories (names starting
with a dot), files that
.Pa .gitignore ,
.Pa .ignore
or
.Pa .rgignore
files list, binary files (any file with a NUL byte), and symbolic links.
Files named on the command line are always searched.
.Pp
Output lines look like
.Ql path:line:text .
The path is left out when the only path is a file or standard input, and the
line number is printed with
.Fl n ,
or by default when standard output is a terminal.
The order of files in the output is not fixed.
.Ss Patterns
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl e Ar pattern , Fl -regexp Ns = Ns Ar pattern
Search for
.Ar pattern .
Repeat to search for lines matching any of several patterns.
With
.Fl e ,
no positional
.Ar pattern
is taken.
.It Fl F , Fl -fixed-strings
Treat patterns as literal text, not regular expressions.
.It Fl i , Fl -ignore-case
Match ASCII letters regardless of case.
.It Fl S , Fl -smart-case
Ignore case unless a pattern contains an upper-case letter.
.It Fl s , Fl -case-sensitive
Match case exactly (the default).
.It Fl w , Fl -word-regexp
Only match whole words, as with
.Ql \eb
around the pattern.
.It Fl x , Fl -line-regexp
Only match whole lines.
.It Fl v , Fl -invert-match
Print the lines that do not match.
.El
.Ss Output
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl n , Fl -line-number
Print line numbers.
.It Fl N , Fl -no-line-number
Do not print line numbers.
.It Fl H , Fl -with-filename
Print the path on every line, even for a single file.
.It Fl I , Fl -no-filename
Never print the path.
.It Fl o , Fl -only-matching
Print each match on its own line instead of the whole line.
.It Fl c , Fl -count
Print the number of matching lines in each file that has any, as
.Ql path:count .
.It Fl l , Fl -files-with-matches
Print only the paths of files with a match.
.It Fl q , Fl -quiet
Print nothing, and stop at the first match.
The exit status says whether anything matched.
.It Fl m Ar num , Fl -max-count Ns = Ns Ar num
Stop reading a file after
.Ar num
matching lines.
.It Fl A Ar num , Fl -after-context Ns = Ns Ar num
Also print
.Ar num
lines after each match.
.It Fl B Ar num , Fl -before-context Ns = Ns Ar num
Also print
.Ar num
lines before each match.
.It Fl C Ar num , Fl -context Ns = Ns Ar num
Both of the above.
Context lines use
.Ql -
instead of
.Ql \&:
after the path and line number, and
.Ql --
separates groups of lines that are not next to each other.
.It Fl -json
Print matching lines, and context lines, as ripgrep's JSON Lines
messages: a
.Ql begin
and an
.Ql end
message around each file's
.Ql match
and
.Ql context
messages, and a
.Ql summary
last.
Text that is not valid UTF-8 is given base64-encoded, as
.Ql bytes .
Lines are numbered unless
.Fl N
is given.
With
.Fl c ,
.Fl l ,
.Fl q
or
.Fl -files
this flag does nothing.
.It Fl 0 , Fl -null
Print a NUL byte after each path instead of
.Ql \&:
(or, with
.Fl l
and
.Fl -files ,
instead of a newline).
.It Fl M Ar num , Fl -max-columns Ns = Ns Ar num
Print a line longer than
.Ar num
bytes, its newline included, as
.Ql [Omitted long matching line]
(or
.Ql [Omitted long context line] ) .
.It Fl -color Ns = Ns Ar when , Fl -colour Ns = Ns Ar when , Fl -heading , Fl -no-heading , Fl -no-messages
Accepted for compatibility and ignored:
.Nm
prints no colors and no headings.
.El
.Ss Files
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -files
Print the files that would be searched, without searching them.
No
.Ar pattern
is taken.
.It Fl g Ar glob , Fl -glob Ns = Ns Ar glob
Only search files matching
.Ar glob ;
with a leading
.Ql \&! ,
leave out files and directories matching it.
Globs use
.Pa .gitignore
syntax
.Pf ( Ql * ,
.Ql ** ,
.Ql \&? ,
.Ql [a-z] ,
.Ql {a,b} )
and match the path as printed.
A glob without a
.Ql /
matches the file name at any depth.
A glob that matches a path takes precedence over ignore files and the
hidden-file rule.
Repeat for several globs; the last one that matches decides.
.It Fl -iglob Ns = Ns Ar glob
Like
.Fl g ,
with ASCII letters matching either case.
.It Fl t Ar type , Fl -type Ns = Ns Ar type
Only search files of
.Ar type ,
ripgrep's file types: a file has a type when its name matches one of the
type's globs
.Pq Fl -type-list .
.Cm all
is any type.
Repeat for several types.
Files named on the command line are searched whatever their type, and a
matching
.Fl g
glob takes precedence.
.It Fl T Ar type , Fl -type-not Ns = Ns Ar type
Do not search files of
.Ar type .
.It Fl -type-list
Print the file types and their globs.
.It Fl \&. , Fl -hidden
Also search hidden files and directories.
.It Fl -no-hidden
Skip hidden files and directories (the default).
.It Fl -no-ignore
Do not use ignore files.
.It Fl -no-ignore-vcs
Do not use
.Pa .gitignore
and
.Pa .git/info/exclude ,
but still use
.Pa .ignore
and
.Pa .rgignore .
.It Fl u , Fl -unrestricted
Once:
.Fl -no-ignore .
Twice:
also
.Fl -hidden .
Three times: also
.Fl -text .
.It Fl a , Fl -text
Search binary files as if they were text.
.It Fl d Ar num , Fl -max-depth Ns = Ns Ar num , Fl -maxdepth Ns = Ns Ar num
Descend at most
.Ar num
directory levels; files directly in a
.Ar path
are at depth 1.
.El
.Ss Other
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl j Ar num , Fl -threads Ns = Ns Ar num
Use
.Ar num
worker threads, or
.Cm auto
(the default): the number of performance cores on Apple silicon, every
logical CPU elsewhere.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print a summary of the options.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Print the version.
.El
.Pp
Options may come before or after the pattern and paths, and short options
combine
.Pf ( Fl nA5
is
.Fl n Fl A Cm 5 ) .
.Fl -
ends the options.
.Sh PATTERN SYNTAX
Patterns are matched against one line at a time, byte by byte; a pattern
cannot contain a newline.
.Bl -tag -width "e{n,m}" -compact
.It Ar x
the byte
.Ar x ;
.Ql \e Ns Ar x
is
.Ar x
literally for any punctuation
.It Li \&.
any byte
.It Li [abc] [^a-z]
one byte from a class, or not;
.Ql \ed \ew \es
and
.Ql [:alpha:]
etc. work inside classes
.It Li \ed \ew \es
an ASCII digit, word byte
.Pf ( Ql [0-9A-Za-z_] ) ,
or white space;
.Ql \eD \eW \eS
are the complements
.It Li \et \er \exNN
tab, carriage return, a byte in hex
.It Li \eb
a word boundary
.It Li ^ $
the start and end of the line
.It Li * + \&?
zero or more, one or more, zero or one; a trailing
.Ql \&?
makes them lazy
.It Li {n} {n,} {n,m}
counted repetition
.It Li a|b
either
.It Li (...) (?:...)
a group
.It Li (?i)
ignore case from here to the end of the group
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev FFS_RG
The path of a ripgrep binary.
A command line
.Nm
can't run itself (multi-line search, sorting, following symbolic links,
any flag it doesn't know) is then run by it, unchanged, so that
.Nm
can stand in for
.Xr rg 1
everywhere.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Something matched (with
.Fl -files ,
some file was listed).
.It 1
Nothing matched.
.It 2
An error: an unknown option, an invalid pattern, or a path that could not
be read.
The other paths are still searched, and the error is printed to standard
error.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
List the build files and READMEs near the top of a project:
.Dl $ ffs --files -g '*README*' -g Makefile -d 2
.Pp
Look for any of several names, with line numbers:
.Dl $ ffs -n 'dictFind|dictAdd|dictDelete' src
.Pp
Read a function body:
.Dl $ ffs -n -A20 '^dictEntry \e*dictFind\e(' src/dict.c
.Pp
Count matching lines per file and show the top three:
.Dl $ ffs -c 'serverLog\e(LL_WARNING' src | sort -t: -k2 -rn | head -3
.Pp
Search the output of a build:
.Dl $ make 2>&1 | ffs 'error:|warning:'
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr grep 1 ,
.Xr rg 1
.Pp
The
.Pa docs
folder of the source repository has a page per topic, with examples.
.Sh CAVEATS
.Fl i
and
.Ql \ew
are ASCII only.
.Ql \eb
treats non-ASCII letters as word characters, as ripgrep does.
A file with a NUL byte is skipped entirely, where ripgrep prints the
matches before the NUL.
Not supported, except through
.Ev FFS_RG :
sorting
.Pq Fl -sort ,
adding file types
.Pq Fl -type-add ,
replacement
.Pq Fl r ,
multi-line search
.Pq Fl U ,
following symbolic links
.Pq Fl L ,
and PCRE syntax
.Pq Fl P .
The global git ignore file is not read.
